        "notebookMd": "# Notebook\n\n## Axial tilt and sunlight angle\n- Seasons are just a thing that happens every year\n- It is summer when Earth is closer to the Sun, and winter when it is farther away.\n- The axis keeps pointing in the same direction in space all year; only Earth's position in its orbit changes.\n- Southern latitudes are negative, and the sign matters when you subtract the declination.\n- Teacher showed me: 40 degrees\n- Earth's axis is tilted 23.5 degrees from the perpendicular to its orbital plane\n- The axis keeps pointing in the same direction in space all year, toward Polaris\n- Seasons are caused by the tilt of the axis, not by Earth's distance from the Sun\n- Earth is closest to the Sun in early January and farthest in early July\n- A high Sun concentrates energy on a small area; a low Sun spreads the same beam over a larger area\n- Noon altitude = 90 - |latitude - declination|, with declination +23.5, 0 and -23.5 on the June solstice, equinoxes and December solstice\n- Sunlight intensity on flat ground is the full beam intensity times the sine of the Sun's altitude\n- The axis keeps pointing in the same direction in space all year; only Earth's position in its orbit changes\n- Southern latitudes are negative, and the sign matters when you subtract the declination\n- Teacher showed me: 5 million km\n",
